export const STATIC_DIAGRAM_PROMPT_VERSION = 'diagram/v7';

/** Source-generation guidance only; visual preferences never become retry conditions. */
export function buildStaticDiagramPrompt(representation: 'svg' | 'html'): string {
  return `Draw one static educational diagram as ${representation}. Return JSON {source}.
Preserve every supplied fact and invariant; invent no numbers, measurements or claims. Quantitative charts and functions use host engines instead. Include short diagram labels, not the page title, teaching paragraphs or repeated page-level hints. Labels bound to graphic positions (vertex letters, side names, lengths, angle labels, axis labels) belong in the diagram; page formulas, derivations, conclusions and step lists are page text and never go into it.
Fit the supplied width/height exactly. SVG requires explicit width, height and viewBox; HTML requires a fixed-size root. Use inline styles and Source Han Sans VF. No JavaScript, external resources, links, images, animation or embedded data. Allowed tags: div span p br b strong i em style svg g path rect circle ellipse line polyline polygon text tspan defs marker linearGradient radialGradient stop clipPath mask title desc.
Design the supplied facts as a coherent illustration:
- Keep the canvas transparent; do not paint a full-canvas background. Leave modest safe margins around content, including water, ground and arrowheads. Avoid accidental empty bands, but let the relation determine the composition rather than stretching it to fill the canvas.
- Design for display at roughly 0.55–0.8 of source size. At a 1280px-wide source, use primary labels around 30–36px and secondary labels at least 24px; scale proportionally for other source widths, never below 22px. Keep labels horizontal and concise.
- Give the main path or axis visual priority with one accent; use supporting tones for secondary structure. Choose colors from designContext.resolvedColors. Prefer balanced 3–5px connector strokes at 1280px width and arrowheads about 3–4 stroke widths long; scale with the source size. Open connectors use fill="none".
- Reserve label space before drawing; paint labels after shapes. Leave clear space between text, unrelated shapes, connectors and other labels. A label may sit inside its own flowchart or structure node box with adequate padding and contrast; do not paint opaque plates behind free labels on the transparent canvas.
- Geometry: compute all coordinates first, then draw. An angle arc is centred on the vertex with both endpoints on the two rays at the same radius; choose the sweep flag so the small arc lies inside the angle. Draw a right-angle square only at the vertex whose angle is 90°, with its sides along the two legs. Offset a side label along its side's outward normal; place vertex labels outside the figure, away from the centroid.
- Solids: use one consistent oblique or isometric projection for the whole figure. Edges that are parallel in space stay parallel in the drawing, hidden edges are dashed, and equal layers or unit cubes are drawn congruent and evenly spaced. Place a dimension label beside its own edge with a short bracket or arrow, and a face label on or beside that face.
- Arrow sizing example for a 4px stroke: <marker id="arrow" markerUnits="userSpaceOnUse" viewBox="0 0 16 12" markerWidth="16" markerHeight="12" refX="15" refY="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L16 6 L0 12 Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker>. Set the arrow fill explicitly to the connector color. With markerUnits="strokeWidth", markerWidth=4 means four stroke widths, not four pixels; never multiply a pixel-sized marker by the stroke width again. Reserve the full arrowhead footprint at endpoints and keep it outside unrelated shapes.
- Allocate a separate rectangle for each label and its secondary line before routing connectors; allow at least 12px clearance at 1280px source width, scaled proportionally. Route around those rectangles, not behind them. Paint order or a label background cannot repair a connector crossing a label. Keep each process label next to its own connection; do not hide the connection under a cloud or another node. Include secondary lines and all arrowheads in the fixed canvas bounds.
- For a timeline, distinguish names, dates and notes; use true numerical spacing or clearly indicate an order-only sequence without quantitative ticks. For a cycle or process, keep directional connections continuous and arrow endpoints clear of labels.
These are design instructions for this source, not a request for a critique or another generation pass.`;
}
